Not in the strong sense implied by the question. A weapon can, under some conditions, deter *some* aggression by making the expected cost of attack unacceptable. That is the core logic of deterrence, and nuclear weapons are the clearest historical example: they may have helped prevent direct great-power war, but they have not abolished war, proxy conflict, crises, proliferation, or the risk of catastrophic accident or miscalculation.

The key point is that deterrence is not a property of a weapon’s mere existence. It is a relational and political condition. It requires:

- the adversary knowing the weapon exists and believing it could be used;
- credible command-and-control and survivability;
- a doctrine linking the weapon to retaliation;
- rational decision-makers on both sides;
- communication and crisis stability;
- and a political context in which the threat is seen as proportionate enough to be believed.

A weapon “so devastating” that it supposedly guarantees peace can also be destabilizing. If it threatens an adversary’s existence, it may create incentives to preempt, proliferate, or adopt “launch on warning” postures. If it is too horrible to use credibly, it may be self-deterring. If only one state has it, others may seek countermeasures; if many have it, accident and escalation risks rise. And “peace” in the negative sense—absence of major war—is not the same as justice, security, or positive peace.

So the defensible claim is narrower: **some devastating weapons can contribute to conditional, fragile deterrence under specific political and technical conditions.** The claim that a single weapon’s mere existence can reliably “promote peace” is not supported by deterrence theory or history. Deterrence can buy time and reduce some risks, but it does not replace diplomacy, institutions, arms control, verification, norms, and crisis management. At best, devastating weapons can make certain wars too costly; they cannot by themselves create durable peace.

